-- Assumptions
1. It is assumed that PHP will executed by system user www-data on the host server. In case it is not, you can replace any reference to user www-data below with the appropriate user in your system.
2. The term directory/directories imply the term folder/folders wherever applicable according to the hosting environment.

-- Application configuration
Copy conf/conf.php.example as conf/conf.php (henceforth called the conf file) and update the conf file according to your environment and need. Configuration keys are documented along side in the conf/conf.php.example file. Check the wiki for more advanced configuration examples.

-- Application sync database creation
1. Create a dedicated database/schema and a database user for this application.
2. Update 'dsn' appropriately in $config['sync_database'] in the conf file. Update database user credentials to an admin user at this stage who can create tables, triggers and sequences in the database/schema created in the above step.
3. Execute 'php src/App/install.php' to create necessary database objects.
4. Now update user credentials in $config['sync_database'] in the conf file to the dedicated database user created for this application. Grant SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE privileges on all tables created in the above step to this user. This user should additionally have USAGE and/or SELECT privileges on all sequences created in the above step.
5. If creating a sqlite database it is recommended to place database file inside the application data directory or provide read-write access to it by the system user www-data.

-- Initializing application sync database
1. Make sure your address books are configured correctly in the conf file before you perform the next steps.
2. Execute 'php src/App/syncdb.php init' to initialize the sync database.

-- Application file/directory permissions
1. Make sure no application files or directories are initially owned by system user www-data.
2. The conf file MUST NOT be world readable but MUST be readable and preferably not writable by system user www-data.
   Equivalent UNIX command: chmod o-rwx conf/conf.php && chmod g-w conf/conf.php && chgrp www-data conf/conf.php
3. Data directory need to be writable by system user www-data and MUST NOT be world readable. All sub directories of data directory need to be writable by system user www-data. Default data directory is data.
   Equivalent UNIX command: chmod o-rwx data && chown -R www-data:www-data data
4. Cache directory need to be writable by system user www-data and MUST NOT be world readable. All sub directories of cache directory need to be writable by system user www-data. Default cache directory is cache.
   Equivalent UNIX command: chmod o-rwx cache && chown -R www-data:www-data cache
5. Temporary files directory need to be writable by system user www-data unless system temporary files directory is configured. Default temporary files directory is tmp.
   Equivalent UNIX command: chmod o-rwx tmp && chown -R www-data:www-data tmp

-- Web server and PHP configuration
Check the wiki for examples.

-- LDAP server
1. Bind user used to search for principals need to have read access to operational attribute entryUUID of all principals.
2. Operational attributes entryUUID, modifyTimestamp and createTimestamp need to be indexed for all contacts.
3. Bind user used for reading/writing contacts in an address book need to have read privileges on operational attributes entryUUID, modifyTimestamp and createTimestamp additionally and must have appropriate size limit and time limit. It is recommended to create a separate user for syncing contacts having only read privileges on operational attributes entryUUID, modifyTimestamp and createTimestamp of the contacts and liberal or no size limit and time limit. This user can be mentioned in 'sync_bind_user' and 'sync_bind_pw' of the address book in the conf file.
